<h2>Evidence That the Harness Moves the Score — Reading Microsoft's White Paper Alongside RHO and HarnessPro</h2>

<h3>Three-line summary</h3>
<ul>
    <li>On November 9, 2026 Microsoft published a white paper that swapped only the harness, keeping model and tasks fixed. Pass rates rose 10.8 points on GPT-5.5 Chat and 22.1 points on Claude Opus 4.8, while swapping the model barely moved them.</li>
    <li>Academia reached the same conclusion by a different road. RHO (City University of Hong Kong and Microsoft Research Asia, EMNLP 2026) rewrote a harness from past execution traces alone, with no ground-truth labels, lifting SWE-Bench Pro from 59% to 78%. Its successor HarnessPro runs that loop recursively, optimizes performance and cost together, and ships three harnesses: Lite, Medium and Heavy.</li>
    <li>All three sources agree on one thing. <strong>With the model held fixed, the variable that moves results by double-digit points is the harness</strong>, and cost can fall even as calls rise, through caching and output compaction. This page puts the three side by side: numbers, structure and limits.</li>
</ul>

<p>A harness is the runtime that surrounds a model: the instructions it receives, the tools it may use, the rule for what happens when a tool fails, and how much of each output is cut before being fed back. <a href="#!hns-overview">What a harness is</a> covers the definition and anatomy, so this page does not repeat them. The interest here is different. In the second half of 2026 the claim that "the harness matters as much as the model, or more" arrived with numbers from both an industry white paper and academic papers. That is too much material to spend on a single news item, so it is written up here as three sources read together.</p>

