<p>The thing to read in these numbers is not the gain itself but its asymmetry. The same harness swap worked nearly twice as hard on Claude Opus 4.8. The paper does not explain this at length, but the published structure is enough. The new harness retried in 84 to 91% of cases after a tool call failed; the Standard harness did so in under 7%. In a long task, one failed tool call used to mean a failed task, and that loss is gone. The better a model handles tools, the more a second chance is worth, so a gain that varies by model is what you would expect.</p>
<p>The cost line also runs against intuition. Model calls roughly doubled, yet the cost of processing input fell 15 to 21%. The paper credits caching. When 83 to 88% of the input in a long conversation is served from cache, more calls add few freshly read tokens. Per-call latency fell 10 to 36% as well. The common belief that "an agent loop is expensive" was a calculation that assumed no cache.</p>

