<p>Microsoft's own selection guide does not present the new harness as universal. Using connectors or MCP servers does not by itself call for the agent loop, and on simple scenarios it can add reasoning, latency and credit consumption without changing the result. As a field example, a resolution agent built on this harness by Microsoft's customer support organization passed a 35% fully autonomous resolution rate in August 2026.</p>

<h3>2. RHO: fixing a harness without answers</h3>
<p>Microsoft's experiment compared two harnesses designed by people. The academic question goes one step further: can the <strong>agent itself</strong> fix the harness, and without ground-truth labels? Earlier methods (prompt optimization, skill and tool synthesis, agent search) all had to score repeatedly against a labeled validation set, which a real deployment does not have. What it does have is the stream of <em>unlabeled execution traces</em> the agent produces every day.</p>
<p>RHO (Retrospective Harness Optimization) is a paper by researchers at City University of Hong Kong and Microsoft Research Asia, posted to arXiv in June 2026 and published in Findings of EMNLP 2026. The method has three stages.</p>

