<p>The first stage picks only a few hard and varied past tasks because of cost: you cannot re-solve everything, so you sample the places where failures cluster. The second stage re-solves those tasks several times in parallel to extract two signals. Within one run, a self-validation signal such as "did the tests pass"; across runs, a self-consistency signal such as "do the answers agree". In the third stage the agent reads that diagnosis, writes several harness edits, re-solves with each, compares each result against the original as a pair, and keeps only the one it judges better. No human grading and no answer key enter anywhere.</p>
<p>The results are for a single round. The comparison set is other label-free methods under the same call budget.</p>
