<p>It was also compared with a method that uses labels. Meta-Harness, which scores against a validation set, reached 0.62 on SWE-Bench Pro at the same single-round budget; RHO reached 0.78 without labels. What RHO writes into the harness is of three kinds: task-agnostic instructions, skills that record environment quirks behind past failures, and executable tools. Each targets one failure mode seen in the original traces.</p>
<p>The paper states its limits. Self-preference is a conservative selector, not a perfect ranker. On SWE-Bench Pro a less-preferred candidate actually scored 0.85, above the 0.78 that was chosen. On no benchmark, though, did the adopted harness fall below the baseline. Part of why the method works on coding agents is that "did the tests pass" is a credible self-signal, so in domains where nothing can be executed, self-preference may wobble. That is the same root as the self-approval bias this wiki discusses in <a href="#!tip-advanced">advanced tips</a>, the tendency of a model to grade its own output generously.</p>
<p>The code is public and is also packaged as a dynamic workflow for Claude Code (<code>retrospection.js</code>). That workflow treats a project's <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, auto-memory and helper scripts as the harness and runs one round from past session transcripts alone. The harness the paper talks about is, for a Claude Code user, a set of files already in hand.</p>

<h3>3. HarnessPro: run it recursively and put cost on the axis</h3>
<p>HarnessPro is a follow-up posted to arXiv in October 2026. Its public repository names the package <code>src/rho/</code> and prefixes environment variables with <code>RHO_</code>, so it is built on RHO's code. Press coverage introduced it as Tsinghua University research, but the arXiv author metadata did not let that affiliation be confirmed, and the paper itself is marked as under review. This page does not assert the affiliation and calls it the RHO follow-up.</p>
<p>Two things differ from RHO. First, it does not stop after one retrospective: diagnose, evolve and select repeat <strong>recursively</strong>. Second, <strong>cost</strong> enters the objective. Optimizing performance alone only ever pushes a harness toward "think more, call more". With cost as the second axis and Pareto selection (keeping only candidates where no axis can improve without the other getting worse), several operating points survive on the frontier. HarnessPro picks three: Lite (cost first), Medium (balanced) and Heavy (performance first).</p>

