<p>The figures are for Codex + GPT-5.5 with the RHO harness as baseline. Heavy: pass rate +2.0 points, cost down 26.1%. Medium: pass rate −1 point, cost down 53%. By benchmark, Heavy averaged 80.8% over three runs on Terminal-Bench 2, 4.5 points above RHO, at 31.7% lower cost (one version of the paper says 33.9%). On SWE-Bench Pro, Heavy scored 80.7% against RHO's 80.0% at 26.5% lower cost. The experiments used two pairings, GPT-5.5 + Codex and GLM-5.2 + Claude Code, on three benchmarks: Terminal-Bench 2, SWE-Bench Pro and ProgramBench.</p>
<p>What an evolved harness actually contains can be read in the repository. Opening the Terminal-Bench 2 harness shows the following.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>A profile selector</strong>: a rubric that reads only the task description and picks one of five profiles, compact, lean, standard, deep or research. Each profile carries a reasoning effort (low, medium, high) and a call budget of the form "economize from the Nth tool call, finalize by the Mth". Lite assigns compact tasks to a small model (GPT-5.4-mini); Heavy promotes standard tasks to high reasoning and leaves room for a final check.</li>
    <li><strong>Output compaction</strong>: a policy that keeps 4,000 characters of each tool output, the first 2,400 and last 900, under a history token cap. A large share of the cost savings comes from here.</li>
    <li><strong>A disabled-feature list</strong>: browser use, multi-agent, plugins, image generation and other features the task does not need are switched off.</li>
    <li><strong>Session instructions</strong>: general rules such as "do not replace a requested implementation with a mock or proxy" and "before finishing, reread the task and check the artifact against independent evidence". The files state that no task IDs or past scores are included.</li>
    <li><strong>Verification helpers</strong>: the SWE-Bench Pro harness ships a <code>repair-verify</code> script that keeps a requirement ledger and runs smoke tests, plus a contract-verification checklist.</li>
</ul>
<p>So a harness the agent wrote for itself is not one long prompt. It is a bundle: a reasoning-effort table, call budgets, output-trimming rules, a list of features to turn off and verification scripts. These sit in the same slots this wiki lays out as the three elements of a harness in <a href="#!hns-kitchen">How it works</a>, now with numbers attached.</p>

<h3>4. Reading the three together</h3>
