<p>The reason for writing this page is that three sources point the same way by different methods. With the model fixed, changing only the harness moves pass rates by double-digit points, and much of the gain comes from operating rules that have nothing to do with model ability: retry after failure, reason only as much as needed, trim outputs before feeding them back. Cost is decided by cache and compaction, not by call count.</p>
<p>Evidence in the other direction belongs next to it. The July Scaffold Effect paper ran two models through three open harnesses (Goose, OpenCode, OpenHands) on 50 Terminal-Bench Pro tasks. Tokens spent per solved task differed by up to 40× across harnesses, while pass-rate differences stayed within 0 to 8 points. Upgrading the model moved token cost only 1.0 to 1.3×. Its conclusion is to report the unit of evaluation as a harness-model pair, with cost alongside. A paper from the same month titled "What Does a Harness Buy? Tokens, Mostly." found that per-task cost differed by up to 3× across harnesses on the same model and tasks. The claim that a harness raises scores and the claim that a harness mostly changes cost do not contradict each other. Depending on which harnesses you compare, the difference shows up as score or as cost, which is why reporting both axes, as HarnessPro does, is a reasonable standard.</p>

<h3>5. What remains for a Claude Code user</h3>
<p>Carried over to Claude Code, these sources leave three things. First, <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, skills, hooks and auto-memory are themselves the harness, and the RHO repository actually ships a workflow that edits those files from past session transcripts. Second, everything HarnessPro evolved (reasoning-effort assignment, tool-output trimming, switching off unneeded features, final verification rules) can be expressed through Claude Code settings and hooks. Third, to claim an effect you have to measure pass rate and tokens together, as the Scaffold Effect paper says. The performance-measurement section of this wiki's <a href="#!hns-usecases">Uses and extensions</a> is where that belongs.</p>

<h3>Fact-check notes</h3>
<p class="scn-sub">Among the Microsoft figures, the pass-rate gains (10.8 and 22.1 points), retry rates after tool failure (84–91% versus under 7%, more than 7×), roughly 2× calls with 15–21% lower input-processing cost, the support agent's 35% autonomous resolution rate and the rename notice's "no change to capabilities or billing" were taken directly from the <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/copilot/blog/copilot-studio/the-github-copilot-harness-in-copilot-studio-is-now-the-microsoft-copilot-harness/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">October 28 rename post</a> and the <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/copilot/blog/copilot-studio/how-the-microsoft-copilot-harness-in-copilot-studio-helps-agents-finish-the-job/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">November 9 post</a>. The 83–88% cache share, 10–36% lower per-call latency and the 65% versus 36% business-process benchmark come from search excerpts of the <a href="https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/copilot-studio-blog/paper-how-utilizing-the-microsoft-copilot-harness-in-copilot-studio-enabled-comp/4571157" target="_blank" rel="noopener">white-paper announcement</a>; the environment this page was written in could not open the white-paper PDF (aka.ms/MCSQuality/Whitepaper) or the Microsoft Learn harness comparison page because of its network policy. The paper names neither the competitor nor the task-set size, and every figure is Microsoft's own measurement. The structural comparison of the two harnesses (wiring, Bash container, subagent role, the warning about simple tasks) uses only what summaries citing Microsoft's official guidance agree on. The August general-availability date rests on one report. RHO's authors, affiliations, venue (Findings of EMNLP 2026, Budapest), three-stage design, results table and Meta-Harness comparison were checked in the cloned <a href="https://github.com/wbopan/retro-harness" target="_blank" rel="noopener">public repository</a> (README, citation file, project page); the "less-preferred candidate reached 0.85" analysis comes from paper excerpts. HarnessPro's three modes, benchmarks, model pairings and Terminal-Bench 2 figures (80.8%, +4.5 points, 31.7% or 33.9%) come from the <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.26770" target="_blank" rel="noopener">arXiv abstract</a> and search excerpts; the harness contents were read directly from the README and policy files of the <a href="https://github.com/pzs19/HarnessPro" target="_blank" rel="noopener">public repository</a>. The +2.0/−26.1%, −1/−53% and SWE-Bench Pro 80.7 versus 80.0 figures are where press coverage and abstract excerpts agree. <strong>The Tsinghua affiliation appears only in press coverage and could not be confirmed from the paper's metadata, so the body does not assert it.</strong> The arXiv full text could not be opened. The Scaffold Effect paper (arXiv 2607.22585; 40×, 0–8 points, 1.0–1.3×) and "What Does a Harness Buy? Tokens, Mostly." (arXiv 2610.04433; up to 3×) are cited from abstract excerpts; the former's reported affiliation, Sentient Labs, rests on a secondary write-up and is not stated in the body.</p>
